A bubble sort sweeps the row, swapping neighbours that are out of order, and repeats until a whole sweep makes no swap. What does the printer output?
public class H044 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {5, 1, 4, 2, 8};
        int passes = 0;
        boolean swapped = true;
        while (swapped) {
            swapped = false;
            passes++;
            for (int i = 0; i < values.length - 1; i++) {
                if (values[i] > values[i + 1]) {
                    int swap = values[i];
                    values[i] = values[i + 1];
                    values[i + 1] = swap;
                    swapped = true;
                }
            }
        }
        System.out.println(passes);
    }
}

A selection sort finds the smallest remaining value in each pass, swaps it into place, and counts the swaps it makes. What does the printer output?
public class H045 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {7, 3, 9, 1};
        int swaps = 0;
        for (int start = 0; start < values.length - 1; start++) {
            int min = start;
            for (int i = start + 1; i < values.length; i++) {
                if (values[i] < values[min]) {
                    min = i;
                }
            }
            if (min != start) {
                int swap = values[start];
                values[start] = values[min];
                values[min] = swap;
                swaps++;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(swaps);
    }
}

An insertion sort takes each value, shifts the larger values on its left one place right, and counts those shifts. What does the printer output?
public class H046 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {3, 1, 2};
        int shifts = 0;
        for (int i = 1; i < values.length; i++) {
            int key = values[i];
            int j = i - 1;
            while (j >= 0 && values[j] > key) {
                values[j + 1] = values[j];
                shifts++;
                j--;
            }
            values[j + 1] = key;
        }
        System.out.println(shifts);
    }
}

A checker must report the single value missing from a dense ascending run, and the run 4, 5, 7, 8 is missing 6 because the step from 5 to 7 skips exactly one value. The loop below prints 3. Which replacement for the assignment inside the branch makes it print 6?
public class H047 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {4, 5, 7, 8};
        int missing = 0;
        for (int i = 0; i < values.length; i++) {
            if (i > 0 && values[i] - values[i - 1] > 1) {
                missing = i + 1;
            }
        }
        System.out.println(missing);
    }
}

A loop carries a balance along the row, and whenever the balance drops below zero it notes the position after the failing value and starts the balance again from there. What does the printer output?
public class H048 {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] values = {2, -3, 4, -1};
        int balance = 0;
